From 2bf9c9187928c40dc3a2fd8beb6c1dd4aba24968 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 06:53:25 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 064/119] scene-referred S1: the exposure constant, unit
 exposure in the tone map, Manual as a pure triangle

Second item of Stage S1. The pre-exposure road built in item 1 now carries the real number, and
the tone map stops applying an exposure of its own in the same commit - either half alone is a
frame of double or zero exposure.

Decisions: scene-notes/decisions/s1-exposure-constant.md approach A (constant) and approach A
(meter form); s1-auto-mode.md part 2 (parameter meanings). Research:
scene-notes/research/s1-exposure-model.md, s1-ccam-exposure-today.md section 5.

- ccam publishes scale = LIGHT_UNIT_SCALE / (1.2 * 2^EV100). The saturation-based film
  sensitivity model is unchanged from CE's own tone mapper and from Frostbite: L_sat =
  (78/(q*S)) * N^2/t = 1.2 * 2^EV100 cd/m2 at ISO 100 with q = 0.65, and engine units are
  1.0 = 10 000 cd/m2, so that scale maps L_sat to 1.0. The derivation of the calibration constant
  is written out at the function: with C = 120*pi and S = 100 a correctly metered scene gives
  2^EV100 = E*100/(120*pi), hence L_sat = E/pi, and an 18 % Lambertian reflector at L = 0.18*E/pi
  lands on exactly 0.18 - the ACES convention the ODT's input domain assumes (D6). The 1.2 is left
  exact because it is 120/S, so ISO keeps meaning ISO; CE's shipped C = 330 is a real dome-receptor
  value that puts mid grey at 0.158 and it stays untouched on the stock path. C only enters where
  the camera METERS, so it appears in this commit as the debug overlay's reference EV and comes
  into force with the AUTO loop in item 4. The research note's numbers were confirmed, not changed.

- The tone map's scene-referred branch is pinned to unit exposure by a CONSTANT, not a shader
  edit: ComputeExposure() returns (LIGHT_UNIT_SCALE/1.2) * 2^-EV100 with EV100 clamped to
  [HDREyeAdaptation.x, .y], so collapsing that window onto EV_unity = log2(LIGHT_UNIT_SCALE/1.2) =
  13.0245867 makes it exactly 1 whatever the meter reports, and .z = 0 removes the :807 scene-key
  tilt with it. HDRFinalPass has exactly one free static flag (SAMPLE0) and S4 needs it for the
  output transform, so spending a permutation to save four ALU would be paying twice. Off the
  switch not one constant moves and HDRPostProcess.cfx is not touched at all.

- The :807 tilt is forced to 0 on the ccam path in both places it can act: the constant above, and
  ccam's own write of E3DPARAM_HDR_EYEADAPTATION_PARAMS (v.z = 0), which today Manual mode silently
  inherits from the time of day at 1.5 - up to +-2 stops of invisible per-scene drift on top of a
  "manual" exposure. That write is no longer what makes the picture, but the EV triple is still
  read by the particle system's ExposureValue domain, so it is kept meaningful: v.x = v.y = the
  camera's EV100.

- Serialisation is untouched. No CRC, member name, enum string or default changed. Under the
  switch Manual is the pure triangle - Exposure Response and Neutral EV are ignored, with one
  CryLog line, because compressing the applied EV around a pivot is a second tone curve and the S
  curve belongs to the output transform (D11). AutoBiased and AutoCompensation have no metering
  loop yet, so FOR THIS ITEM they expose as Manual and log once; item 4 gives them the meanings in
  the decision table (AUTO with EV bias = manualEV - NeutralEV, and AUTO with EC). Off the switch
  all three modes behave exactly as they always did.

- With Scene Referred requested the camera owns the exposure whether or not Enable Exposure
  Control is ticked: the tone map applies unity on that path, so a camera that published nothing
  would render raw scene radiance and the frame would be black. Off the switch the tick keeps its
  old meaning exactly.

- The duplicated stops arithmetic is unified. ComputeApertureShutterStops() = log2(T^2/t) is now
  the one definition of what the aperture and shutter let through (transmission and bokeh-mask
  blocking included); ComputeManualEV100() and the film-grain light term both call it instead of
  computing it separately, so grain and exposure can no longer drift.

- The effective-T-stop diagnostic's edge-trigger state moved from function statics to per-component
  members, as did the two new log-once flags: statics are shared by every cinematic camera in the
  level.

- r_HDRDebug 1 grows a scene-referred branch that reports what actually happened - the applied
  EV100 recovered from the latched pre-exposure, the metered EV at C = 120*pi, and the raw scale.
  The gap between the two EVs is the exposure error, which is exactly the quantity AUTO will drive
  to zero.

Built clean (0 errors, 0 warnings outside Code/SDKs): CryRenderD3D11, CinematicCamera.

